> Philip introduced himself by saying, “Look around you, Jesus, what do you see? What do you think you see?” “I see my father’s garden,” Jesus replied, perplexed. Philip continued, “I see the distant Mediterranean Sea that brought me here that provides the water nature craves, transported to this parched land by the clouds, that falls as rain. What do you eat, Jesus?” “We bake our own bread,” Jesus replied. “When you bake it, do you see the heat of thousands of suns remembered by the wood and returned to you?” Jesus was taken aback, he recounted to me later. He had never heard someone talk in this way. The conversation morphed into a long walk in his father’s olive groves under the full moon.
> 
> The next day, Philip began what would prove to be an even longer discourse, “No one knows what came before, but we have our own sacred texts that tell the myths of my land. Tales of the gods, the immortals and kings, preserved in the poems of Homer and the histories of Herodotus. One poem, *The Iliad*, read by young Greeks to build character, tells of a campaign to the kingdom of Troy where a warrior, Achilles, reputed to be the son of a god, after an epic siege brought down a great kingdom and his name became immortal. These tales are a jumble of things we accept and things we cannot understand, but they exemplify the qualities it takes to be Greek and give us our identity.”
